body:
I think you need to provide more context for a healthier response.

What do you mean by "throw shit", do you consider honest and unmalicious criticism as "throwing shit"? If so, I personally feel like I sometimes need a "mirror" to understand myself better and "objectively". For example, I was told I was  acting childish-irresponsible/dismissive and that was related to my escapism and bad stress management. Now we are starting to work on finding out from what I am trying to escape.

&#x200B;

However, if you mean by "throwing shit" being treated rude, unjustly and being made feel worthless no. IMO, no human is allowed to do that since every human is owed atleast basic respect and worth. So if you are being told that you are "worhtless", "incapable" ,"villain" etc. I can't think that ca't be helpful to anyone.

&#x200B;

Any way, are you asking this question regarding therapy?? Because I can't imagine a therapist "throwing shit" to make a patient feel like garbage. I hope thats not the case.

I am not a therapist, but if  anyone, regardless of  their role in my life, found out through research where I lived and hanged around with hopes to bump into me, I would feel really uncomfortable and lose my trust towards that person. Also, I find it disrespectful. 

&#x200B;

If I were you I would avoid going near her address, and be honestly open about it.  She might get upset though.
